1
00:00:01,320 --> 00:00:08,330
OpenStreetMap is the Wikipedia of maps and so it's a map that citizens contribute to.

2
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:11,679
It's a map that appears on the website
OpenStreetMap.org but then you can edit it.

3
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:16,039
What OpenStreetMap really is, it's a
citizen generated database

4
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:19,609
of geographical information. This is very
different to the way Google organizes

5
00:00:19,609 --> 00:00:24,880
itself, they have a set of map products.
Google's business is more about places

6
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:29,099
where there is commercially valuable
information, where there is revenue to be had.

7
00:00:29,099 --> 00:00:33,570
They never publish the raw data
that makes up the map, the coordinates of

8
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:37,440
the streets for example, all of that data is
kept locked away

9
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,920
in Google's case, because that's their commercial advantage.

10
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:46,520
Organizations like the Ordnance Survey, here in the UK, they have always had

11
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:52,540
raw map data which they will license to other organizations, at great cost. So
OpenStreetMap

12
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:56,040
was formed here in the UK actually out of frustration.

13
00:00:56,050 --> 00:01:00,969
It was a surprise to the people who started the project actually there are a lot of

14
00:01:00,969 --> 00:01:06,500
people out there that really wanted Open
License map data so badly that they are

15
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,530
willing to do this crazy thing of going
out in the street with the GPS unit trying

16
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:11,930
to build a map from scratch.

17
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:15,110
We are trying to empty our minds of
other maps because you don't want to be

18
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:18,960
accused of copying, we were trying to create a brand new map which was open licensed,

19
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:22,450
but we're gonna fill in streets and
gather the street names and all that

20
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:23,820
very basic data, and then

21
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:29,070
over time here in London we've seen all
all the streets mapped out in a lot of detail,

22
00:01:29,070 --> 00:01:33,530
but that's just London of course and we've seen the coverage of the map spreading

23
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:38,570
but you get these wonderful blossoms of
detail appearing as just one person gets

24
00:01:38,570 --> 00:01:40,560
interested in it.

25
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:44,479
Unfortunately we do have an uneven level
of coverage, this is one of the

26
00:01:44,479 --> 00:01:48,119
challenges of working with OpenStreetMap data,

27
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:52,680
there's no guarantee that a particular level of detail has been reached everywhere.

28
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:55,820
We are consumers of maps in order primarily to find out

29
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:59,880
where patients are and where their greatest needs are, and if we

30
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,250
find that an area of tremendous
vulnerability and where there's a lot of

31
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:06,509
humanitarian and medical need and
interest, that there isn't sufficient

32
00:02:06,509 --> 00:02:10,369
base mapping well there's nothing
stopping us from contributing it.

33
00:02:10,369 --> 00:02:14,239
The canonical example of humanitarian
OpenStreetMap work was in Haiti after

34
00:02:14,239 --> 00:02:17,820
the earthquake, when there was an
incredible rash of volunteer mapping that

35
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:22,590
happened, and it created an extraordinary
detailed map of the Capital Area Port-au-Prince.

36
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:26,860
Everyone was sitting at home watching the news, watching the disastrous earthquake

37
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:31,150
in Haiti and I think a lot of the OSM

38
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:36,560
community sort of naturally felt curious to
see how much data we had, how is the

39
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,220
map looking in Haiti?

40
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:43,700
it was looking very bare, we had a few roads in place, and so we were

41
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:48,189
thinking, well maybe we should try and boost the coverage of of the cities

42
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,940
where this disaster has struck. People
just started contributing to the map in that area.

43
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:59,100
This is a process of remote mapping so looking at the aerial imagery that we have available to us.

44
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:01,400
When we had our patients coming to the cholera centers

45
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,060
and they tell us where they're from

46
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:07,250
because of the OpenStreetMap work that had been done, we were able actually correlate

47
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:10,780
those neighborhoods that they gave us as
their origins to actual places and

48
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,599
figure out where more patients were
coming from and where for example there were

49
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:16,440
water outages, which we were then able to help correct.

50
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,740
So in that sense the map actually helped to save lives.

51
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:26,069
Humanitarian OSM work has been done in the Haitian earthquake, the ebola disaster

52
00:03:26,069 --> 00:03:30,239
that just happened, the Nepal earthquake,
the Philippines typhoon and all of these

53
00:03:31,079 --> 00:03:35,870
places increasingly as time goes on, it's the default map and it's happening with

54
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:40,199
volunteers all over the world. But it's
still very much also about on the ground

55
00:03:40,199 --> 00:03:44,410
surveys, so we are encouraging the
community to go out and look at the real

56
00:03:44,410 --> 00:03:49,299
world with their own eyes and that's how
you can really create a unique valuable

57
00:03:49,299 --> 00:03:53,040
data set that really has a sense of ownership
with the community living in a local area.

58
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:57,010
So increasingly we're starting to encourage our field teams to collect

59
00:03:57,010 --> 00:03:57,920
that geographical data about their

60
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:03,560
activities and put it on OpenStreetMap. We
generally ask permission

61
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,750
you know, do you mind if we put your
village on the map? Invariably what

62
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:12,020
people say 'yes we'd like to be on the map,
we'd like for people to know that we exist.'

63
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:17,100
Every month we have a mapathon in London
which attracts 75 to 100 people and they

64
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:22,410
sit down and they trace some area, last
month we were doing Chad. We bring 100 people

65
00:04:22,410 --> 00:04:25,760
and some pizzas, and maybe a few bottles of
beer or wine and everyone sits down and

66
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:29,780
looks at the satellite imagery and traces
the things that are necessary.

67
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,700
We need you to trace the houses here, we need the road
network there, we need the water courses

68
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:36,700
but it's actually contributing
meaningfully in real time to actual

69
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,380
humanitarian operations.

70
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:43,450
In a way OpenStreetMap could be thought of delivering
real power to developers and powering an

71
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:47,540
ecosystem of different third parties doing really
interesting stuff with OpenStreetMap data.

72
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,940
And there's still lots of niches
for people to organise that data. There are

73
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:55,710
several companies that actually make
quite a good living organizing and

74
00:04:55,710 --> 00:05:00,380
presenting the OpenStreetMap data very
usefully so

75
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:05,640
I don't think having all this data out
there in an open format is going to

76
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,270
destroy business opportunities,

77
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:08,940
quite the contrary I think it's going to
create them.